# LCD display configuration. These symbols exist only when spangap-lcd is in the
# staged set — i.e. the buildable requires it and `spangap build --no-lcd` was
# not passed; presence of the straddle is the enable, there is no on/off symbol
# here. A board sets the pins in its sdkconfig.defaults; tweak per-build with
# `spangap menuconfig`.
#
# The display itself — SPI bus, panel controller, backlight, orientation — is
# described entirely below and brought up by the lcd component (lcd_panel.cpp),
# so a board with a standard SPI panel contributes no display C code. A standard
# I2C touch controller is likewise described below and brought up by the
# component (lcd_touch.cpp). Bespoke input (a trackball, buttons, a touch
# controller a board samples on its own task) stays behind lcd_input.h.

menu "spangap: spangap-lcd"

# ─── font engine ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How lcdFont() (lcd_fonts.cpp) turns a (face, px) request into an lv_font_t.
# The vector engines render the TTFs shipped to /fixed/fonts on the device, so
# any size is data + arithmetic; BITMAP keeps today's compiled-in bitmaps.
choice LCD_FONT_ENGINE
    prompt "On-device font engine"
    default LCD_FONT_FREETYPE
    help
        FREETYPE (default): hinted, CJK-capable vector rendering via
        espressif/freetype (~400 KB code). TINY_TTF: ~25 KB code, no hinting
        (soft below ~14 px), no synthetic bold/italic — the small-image option.
        BITMAP: no engine; lcdFont() maps to the nearest compiled bitmap font
        (today's behaviour, smallest flash).
    config LCD_FONT_FREETYPE
        bool "FreeType (vector, hinted)"
        select LV_USE_FREETYPE
    config LCD_FONT_TINY_TTF
        bool "tiny_ttf (vector, tiny)"
        select LV_USE_TINY_TTF
    config LCD_FONT_BITMAP
        bool "Bitmap only (compiled-in)"
endchoice

config LCD_FONT_MIN_VECTOR_PX
    int "Smallest px rendered as vector (below → pixel font; 0 = always vector)"
    default 0
    depends on !LCD_FONT_BITMAP
    help
        A request smaller than this many pixels falls back to a hand-tuned
        bitmap font (tomthumb/spleen by height) instead of a soft vector glyph.
        Default 0 disables the fallback — everything renders as vector, however
        small. Set to ~10 to dial the bitmap floor back in for tiny text.

# ─── panel transport ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which wires the pixels leave on. The two are different enough that they are
# different files (lcd_panel.cpp / lcd_panel_rgb.cpp), each compiling to nothing
# when the other is chosen; everything above lcdPanelInit is shared.
choice LCD_BUS
    prompt "Panel transport"
    default LCD_BUS_SPI
    help
        SPI: a controller with its own GRAM (ST7789, ILI9341), written a strip
        at a time over a bus it may share. RGB: a parallel panel with no memory
        of its own, refreshed continuously by the SoC out of a PSRAM
        framebuffer, which is what a 480x640 panel on an ESP32-S3 is.

    config LCD_BUS_SPI
        bool "SPI (esp_lcd panel-io)"
    config LCD_BUS_RGB
        bool "RGB parallel, 16-bit"
endchoice

# ─── shared SPI bus ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# spi_helper makes bus bring-up idempotent, so the panel can share these pins
# with the SD card and a LoRa modem (as on the T-Deck); only CS/DC are the
# panel's own.
config LCD_SPI_HOST
    int "SPI host (1=SPI1 2=SPI2/FSPI 3=SPI3)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default 2
    help
        The peripheral name, matching the LORA_/SDCARD_ host knobs. The IDF
        spi_host_device_t enum is offset by one (SPI2_HOST=1); lcd_panel.cpp
        subtracts it.

config LCD_SCK_PIN
    int "SPI SCK pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1
config LCD_MOSI_PIN
    int "SPI MOSI pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1
config LCD_MISO_PIN
    int "SPI MISO pin (-1 if unused)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1

# ─── panel ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
config LCD_CS_PIN
    int "Panel chip-select pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1
config LCD_DC_PIN
    int "Panel data/command pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1
config LCD_RST_PIN
    int "Panel reset pin (-1 if it resets with the power rail)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default -1
config LCD_BL_PIN
    int "Backlight pin — LEDC PWM (-1 if none / not host-controlled)"
    default -1
config LCD_PCLK_MHZ
    int "Pixel clock in MHz"
    default 40 if LCD_BUS_SPI
    default 16
    help
        On SPI: pins not on the SoC's SPI IOMUX route via the GPIO matrix, which
        caps the ESP32-S3 SPI clock around 40 MHz — asking for more just clamps.

        On RGB it is the panel's refresh rate in disguise: frame rate =
        pclk / (htotal × vtotal), and every pixel of every frame is read out of
        PSRAM, so this is also the bandwidth the rest of the device has to share
        with. Raising it past what the memory can feed shows as a torn or
        drifting image, not as a slow one.

choice
    prompt "Panel controller"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default LCD_CONTROLLER_ST7789
    help
        The display controller. ST7789 is built into esp_lcd (no extra
        dependency); ILI9341 is the espressif/esp_lcd_ili9341 managed component,
        pulled in automatically. Both are RGB565-over-SPI parts driven through
        the same esp_lcd panel ops, so the runtime path is shared.

        An RGB panel's controller is not named here: it has no esp_lcd driver in
        this straddle at all. Its registers are written once, before its RGB
        timing starts, over whatever side channel the board gave it — a 3-wire
        SPI whose chip-select is often on an IO expander — which makes that
        sequence board code, in the board's own onStart.
    config LCD_CONTROLLER_ST7789
        bool "ST7789 / ST7789V"
    config LCD_CONTROLLER_ILI9341
        bool "ILI9341"
endchoice
config LCD_CONTROLLER_ID
    int
    default 0 if LCD_CONTROLLER_ST7789
    default 1 if LCD_CONTROLLER_ILI9341
    default 0

# ─── RGB parallel panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 16-bit RGB565: five blue, six green, five red data lines plus the four sync
# signals, all driven by the SoC's LCD_CAM peripheral straight out of a
# framebuffer. There is no controller driver and no panel-io — see the note in
# the controller choice above.
config LCD_RGB_HSYNC_PIN
    int "RGB HSYNC pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_VSYNC_PIN
    int "RGB VSYNC pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_DE_PIN
    int "RGB DE (data enable) pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_PCLK_PIN
    int "RGB PCLK pin"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1

# Data lines, in the panel's own naming: B0..B4, G0..G5, R0..R4 (RGB565's five
# blue, six green, five red). A 5-bit channel's B0/R0 is the LSB the panel does
# not bring out on many 18-bit glasses wired for 16 — those stay -1 only if the
# board really leaves them unconnected, which is not the same as being unused.
config LCD_RGB_B0_PIN
    int "RGB data B0"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_B1_PIN
    int "RGB data B1"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_B2_PIN
    int "RGB data B2"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_B3_PIN
    int "RGB data B3"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_B4_PIN
    int "RGB data B4"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G0_PIN
    int "RGB data G0"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G1_PIN
    int "RGB data G1"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G2_PIN
    int "RGB data G2"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G3_PIN
    int "RGB data G3"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G4_PIN
    int "RGB data G4"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_G5_PIN
    int "RGB data G5"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_R0_PIN
    int "RGB data R0"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_R1_PIN
    int "RGB data R1"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_R2_PIN
    int "RGB data R2"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_R3_PIN
    int "RGB data R3"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1
config LCD_RGB_R4_PIN
    int "RGB data R4"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default -1

# Blanking intervals, in pixel clocks (horizontal) and lines (vertical). They
# come from the panel's data sheet, and a panel run outside them shows a rolling
# or offset image rather than nothing at all.
config LCD_RGB_HSYNC_PULSE
    int "HSYNC pulse width (pclk)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 8
config LCD_RGB_HSYNC_BACK
    int "Horizontal back porch (pclk)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 50
config LCD_RGB_HSYNC_FRONT
    int "Horizontal front porch (pclk)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 10
config LCD_RGB_VSYNC_PULSE
    int "VSYNC pulse width (lines)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 8
config LCD_RGB_VSYNC_BACK
    int "Vertical back porch (lines)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 20
config LCD_RGB_VSYNC_FRONT
    int "Vertical front porch (lines)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 10
config LCD_RGB_PCLK_ACTIVE_NEG
    bool "Latch data on the falling PCLK edge"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default n
    help
        Which edge the glass samples on. Wrong, the image is there but smeared
        or noisy at the pixel level rather than absent — try the other one.

config LCD_RGB_DRAW_LINES
    int "Draw strip height in lines"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 80
    range 8 240
    help
        How much of the panel LVGL renders per flush, into a PSRAM strip the
        flush then copies into the framebuffer. Unlike the SPI strip this is not
        bounded by a bus transfer — it is a straight trade of PSRAM (width ×
        lines × 2 bytes) against the number of times a repaint stops to copy.

        ONE framebuffer. The panel is scanning out the same memory the flush is
        writing into, so a repaint large enough to race the scan can be seen
        arriving. A second framebuffer to swap on VSYNC is what removes that,
        and it costs another width × height × 2 (600 KB at 480x640) plus an
        LVGL render mode this straddle does not yet drive.

config LCD_RGB_BOUNCE_LINES
    int "Bounce-buffer height in lines (0 = DMA straight from PSRAM)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_RGB
    default 0
    range 0 64
    help
        Non-zero puts two small internal-RAM buffers between PSRAM and the
        panel, refilled by the driver as the frame is scanned out. That costs
        2 × lines × width × 2 bytes of the internal heap and some CPU, and buys
        tolerance of the moments the PSRAM bus is not available to the LCD DMA —
        most of all a flash write, which is when a straight-from-PSRAM panel
        shows a band of noise. Start at 0 and raise it if the screen tears while
        the device writes its store.

# ─── touch (standard I2C controllers) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Brought up by the component (lcd_touch.cpp) the same way the panel is: a
# board with one of these parts contributes no touch C code, only pins. Raw
# points are reported in NATIVE panel coordinates and oriented with the same
# rotation/mirror as the pixels (lcdPanelOrientTouch). When NONE is selected,
# the board HAL's lcd_input.h touch_read (if any) drives touch instead — that
# fallback is for a controller the component can't own, e.g. one sampled on a
# board task to share its I2C bus with other board peripherals (the T-Deck).
choice
    prompt "Touch controller"
    default LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
    help
        The panel's capacitive touch controller, driven through esp_lcd_touch.
        NONE for a board without touch, or one whose board HAL reads its own
        controller through lcd_input.h.
    config LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
        bool "None / board HAL"
    config LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_FT5X06
        bool "FT5x06 / FT6x36 family"
    config LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_GT911
        bool "GT911"
endchoice

config LCD_TOUCH_I2C_PORT
    int "Touch I2C port"
    default 0
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
    help
        The component creates this I2C master port itself, unless
        LCD_TOUCH_I2C_ADOPT says the board already did — so on a board using
        this block without that, the touch controller must be the port's only
        creator.

config LCD_TOUCH_I2C_ADOPT
    bool "Adopt the bus the board already created on that port"
    default n
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
    help
        For a board whose touch controller shares its wires with other chips —
        an IO expander, an RTC, an IMU — and which therefore brings that bus up
        itself, in its start-band service, before the display comes up. The
        component then adds the controller as one more device on the existing
        bus instead of putting a second master on the same two wires, which the
        SoC allows and the wires do not. The pins above still describe the
        wiring; they are simply not used to create anything.
config LCD_TOUCH_I2C_SDA
    int "Touch I2C SDA pin"
    default -1
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
config LCD_TOUCH_I2C_SCL
    int "Touch I2C SCL pin"
    default -1
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
config LCD_TOUCH_I2C_KHZ
    int "Touch I2C clock (kHz)"
    default 100
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
config LCD_TOUCH_INT_PIN
    int "Touch interrupt pin"
    default -1
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
    help
        Wired to the shared input ISR: an edge wakes the lcd task, which reads
        the controller and keeps re-reading at ~10 ms while a finger is down.
        The indevs are event-mode (no polling), so without an INT line touch
        never fires — -1 is only for a board with no touch routed.
config LCD_TOUCH_RST_PIN
    int "Touch reset pin (-1 if it resets with the power rail)"
    default -1
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE

# How the touch glass is mounted relative to the panel — a lamination fact, not
# a display one, so it is corrected on the raw point BEFORE the panel's rotation
# is applied. LCD_MIRROR_X/Y below move the pixels with the points and so can
# never fix touch alone. Swap first (it brings the glass's axes onto the panel's
# native ones), then the mirrors, which are taken against the native maxes.
config LCD_TOUCH_SWAP_XY
    bool "Touch swap X/Y (glass mounted across the panel's axes)"
    default n
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
config LCD_TOUCH_MIRROR_X
    bool "Touch mirror X (a touch left of centre reads right of it)"
    default n
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE
config LCD_TOUCH_MIRROR_Y
    bool "Touch mirror Y (a touch above centre reads below it)"
    default n
    depends on !LCD_TOUCH_CONTROLLER_NONE

config LCD_WAKE_ON_TOUCH_DEFAULT
    bool "Touch wakes the device from standby, by default"
    default n
    help
        The shipped value of s.lcd.wake_on_touch, which the operator can flip
        on the Display page. It is the CASE that decides which way round it
        belongs: a deck carried in a pocket wants its button to be the only way
        back (n — a bag full of touches would keep lighting the screen), while a
        handheld whose button is round the back is expected to wake where it is
        touched (y). Seeded on any board with touch, this controller's or a
        board HAL's; the wake source itself is armed only while in standby.

# ─── geometry / orientation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Native = the panel's physical pixels, before rotation. Resolution can't be
# probed from an SPI panel (the controller's GRAM doesn't report the attached
# glass), so it must be set here. Rotation is done in hardware (swap_xy + mirror)
# at bring-up and the post-rotation size is what LVGL is told; the same transform
# is applied to raw touch (lcdPanelOrientTouch) so touch and pixels always agree.
config LCD_NATIVE_WIDTH
    int "Native panel width (px, pre-rotation)"
    default 240
config LCD_NATIVE_HEIGHT
    int "Native panel height (px, pre-rotation)"
    default 320

choice
    prompt "Rotation"
    default LCD_ROTATION_90
    help
        Hardware rotation applied at bring-up. The base swap_xy/mirror per
        rotation follows the common ST7789/ILI9341 table; if a panel's scan
        direction differs the image comes out mirrored — use the Mirror X/Y
        toggles below to correct it.

        An RGB panel has no rotation to apply: the SoC scans the framebuffer out
        in the order the glass reads it, so only 0 and 180 exist there (180
        being both mirrors) and the quarter turns are refused at bring-up with
        a log line rather than rendered in software.
    config LCD_ROTATION_0
        bool "0° (portrait, native)"
    config LCD_ROTATION_90
        bool "90° (landscape)"
    config LCD_ROTATION_180
        bool "180° (portrait, flipped)"
    config LCD_ROTATION_270
        bool "270° (landscape, flipped)"
endchoice
config LCD_ROTATION
    int
    default 0   if LCD_ROTATION_0
    default 90  if LCD_ROTATION_90
    default 180 if LCD_ROTATION_180
    default 270 if LCD_ROTATION_270
    default 0

config LCD_MIRROR_X
    bool "Mirror X (flip if the image is left-right reversed)"
    default n
config LCD_MIRROR_Y
    bool "Mirror Y (flip if the image is top-bottom reversed)"
    default n
config LCD_INVERT_COLOR
    bool "Invert colour (most ST7789 IPS panels need this)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default y

config LCD_DRAW_STRIP_KB
    int "Draw strip size (KB of internal DMA RAM)"
    depends on LCD_BUS_SPI
    default 8
    range 2 32
    help
        How much of the panel is rendered and sent per SPI transfer. This is
        the redraw you can SEE: a repaint costs (area / strip) transfers, each
        with a bus lock, a DMA setup and a completion wait, so a small strip
        makes a keypress repaint crawl down its keyboard.

        The ceiling on this is not the panel, it is INTERNAL DMA-CAPABLE RAM,
        which the display does not own: the SPI driver allocates a private DMA
        buffer from the same heap for any transaction whose buffer is not
        DMA-capable (every SD read into a PSRAM cache), later than us and under
        WiFi, and a failure there takes the device down inside the driver. 8 KB
        is 12 lines of a 320-wide panel and costs exactly what the two 4 KB
        double buffers used to; going higher is real, and wants measuring
        against `free` on the device rather than hoping. Bring-up halves this
        until the allocation leaves a reserve standing, so an over-ambitious
        value degrades to a stripier screen instead of a panic. Never above the
        bus ceiling, SPANGAP_SPI_MAX_TRANSFER.

# ─── UI scale ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
config LCD_UI_SCALE_DEFAULT
    int "Default UI zoom (%)"
    default 100
    range 50 250
    help
        The shipped value of s.lcd.scale, which the operator changes on the
        Display page. ONE number scales the whole shell: every length the UI
        states goes through lcdPx() and every font size through the stylesheet's
        token resolution, both multiplied by exactly this.

        It is a property of the GLASS, not of the panel's pixel count: 100 means
        the 320x240 sheet at its own size, which on a ~143 ppi 2.8" panel is what
        the shell was drawn against. A panel of the same physical size at twice
        that density wants ~200 to feel identical and less than that to trade
        size for content — the Waveshare 2.8B ships 150. Set it from the pixel
        pitch, then look at the thing.

# ─── settings UI ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
config LCD_SETTINGS_MARQUEE
    bool "Marquee long settings values on focus"
    default y
    help
        In the Settings pane, a long read-only value (identity / destination
        hashes, paths) is shown on a single line, ellipsized, and made keypad-
        focusable: when you land on it with the trackball/keypad it horizontally
        scrolls (LVGL LV_LABEL_LONG_SCROLL_CIRCULAR — Brookesia-watch style),
        only the focused row, then re-ellipsizes when you move off. Turn this off
        on touch-only boards to keep the older behaviour (long values wrap onto a
        second line, tap-to-read) — those boards have no focus ring to drive the
        marquee.

endmenu
